\section{Introduction}
Typography is the art and technique of arranging type to make written language legible, readable, and appealing when displayed. The arrangement of type involves selecting typefaces, point sizes, line lengths, line-spacing, and letter-spacing, and adjusting the space between pairs of letters (kerning).

In the realm of mathematics, typography becomes crucial for ensuring clarity in complex expressions. Consider the fundamental relation between energy, mass, and the speed of light:
\begin{equation}
  E = mc^2
\end{equation}
where \( E \) represents energy, \( m \) represents mass, and \( c \) is the speed of light in a vacuum\footnote{The value of \( c \) is approximately \( 299,792,458 \) m/s.}.

